text: |-
  1984 @TheOfficial1984 • 7h
  TLDR version..
  O...
  THE @ADL IS SPYING ON YOU.
  ISRAELI INTELLIGENCE IS SPYING ON YOU.
  THEY ARE MANIPULATING YOUR FEED, AND YOUR MIND, USING BOTS,
  AND AI BEHOLDEN TO A HOSTILE FOREIGN GOVERNMENT THAT WANTS
  TO KILL YOU.
  X IS THE WILD WEST.
  Yall have a great day now
  Executive Summary
  The query references the Anti-Defamation League's (ADL)
  partnerships with tech firms like Cyabra and CHEQ-
  analyzed in prior reports—as potential privacy risks, viewed
  through the lens of ADL's documented history of surveillance
  and infiltration activities. This analysis confirms that ADL's
  past operations, spanning decades, involved extensive
  spying on activists, civil rights groups, and critics of Israel,
  often in collaboration with law enforcement and informants.
  These actions, exposed in scandals like the 1993 FBI
  investigation, raise legitimate concerns about data handling
  in modern partnerships, where ADL accesses social media
  APIs and shares intelligence on "hate" content. While Cyabra
  and CHEQ emphasize GDPR/CCPA compliance and
  anonymized processing, the absence of third-party audits,
  combined with ADL's opaque data flows, amplifies risks of
  mission creep—e.g., flagging dissent as "disinformation." As
  of November 1, 2025, no major breaches have been linked to
  these specific ties, but ethical precedents suggest
  heightened scrutiny for users whose data may be indirectly
  profiled.
  5
